{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774743,"nonce":"g0JgvaSQNj2SpeWoA0MTyTtt34Q=","title":"chore: stop tracking .envrc — it is per-developer","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #90 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `chore/pin-kube-context-to-colima` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-26T14:04:24Z · Merged: 2026-07-26T17:35:28Z\n\nWhat has to be ambient in a shell depends on the machine, not the repo: which local cluster exists, which kube contexts sit alongside it, where a toolchain lives. Tracking one developer's `.envrc` pushes those local answers onto everyone who clones.\n\nConcretely, the thing that prompted this: this machine's kubeconfig lists several production EKS clusters beside `colima`, so a local `.envrc` here prepends a two-line overlay to `KUBECONFIG` pinning the repo to colima (`current-context` is honoured from the first file in the list, so nothing is copied and nothing goes stale when colima is recreated). That is the right guard for *that* setup and meaningless on a machine without colima — exactly the kind of thing that should not be committed.\n\n\n- `git rm --cached .envrc` — **the file stays on disk** for whoever has one; only tracking stops.\n- Gitignore `.envrc`. Note the existing `.env.*` rule does **not** cover it: that pattern needs a literal dot after `env`.\n- Gitignore `/deploy/local-helm/.kubeconfig-colima`, the kube-context overlay a local `.envrc` may generate. Holds no credentials, but it is machine-local state and must not exist before the local cluster does.\n\nVerified: both paths are ignored, and `direnv exec` still resolves the context to `colima` locally — untracking changes nothing about how the file works.\n\n\nThe tracked `.envrc` carried real rationale: why `SCARAB_TEST_DATABASE_URL` has to be ambient (an IDE-launched test never goes through `just test`, and without the URL the Postgres-backed tests skip for a reason that looks like a code bug), and why `.env.local` must never be dotenv'd into every shell — a rule `deploy/local-helm/deploy.sh`'s hardening block cites by name.\n\nIf that guidance should survive for fresh clones it wants a `.envrc.example` or a home in `docs/`. This PR deliberately does not decide that.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}